<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  firewalld service definition for OmniBridge. Installed as
  /usr/lib/firewalld/services/omnibridge.xml by the `omnibridge` core package.

  Shipped, never enabled
  ======================
  Installing this file adds nothing to any zone. It gives the user a service
  named "omnibridge" to add with `firewall-cmd`, instead of a port number to
  remember; the exact command is in packaging/fedora/README.md, because an XML
  comment cannot contain a long option without breaking the parser. No
  scriptlet in this package runs `firewall-cmd`, on install,
  upgrade or removal: a package that silently opens a port is doing something
  the user did not ask for, and one that silently closes a port on uninstall is
  removing a rule the user added by hand. Readiness audit §9.2 and §9.4.

  On Fedora Workstation nothing needs doing at all — the default zone already
  permits both flows (MEASURED, audit §4.6). This file is for `public`,
  `FedoraServer`, and anyone who has tightened their own zone.

  TCP 55432 only
  ==============
  mDNS is deliberately absent. firewalld already ships `mdns.xml`, correctly
  scoped to 224.0.0.251 and ff02::fb; redeclaring UDP 5353 here would be a
  second definition to keep right and a broader rule than the stock one. The
  description tells the user to enable firewalld's own service instead.

  Nothing else is opened. No port range, no outbound rule, no forwarding.
-->
<service>
  <short>OmniBridge</short>
  <description>Local-first device continuity between this computer and your phone. Peer sessions arrive on TCP 55432. Discovery uses mDNS, which this service deliberately does not cover: enable firewalld's own "mdns" service for that.</description>
  <port protocol="tcp" port="55432"/>
</service>
